1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Prepare for takeoff.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Extreme aircraft of all kinds are maneuvering for position in the skies of the 21st century.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
From a fighter jet straight out of Star Wars,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and a supersonic bomber that thinks it's a fighter,

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
to miniature eyes in the skies,

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:23,000
personal-sized flying machines,

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
and jumbo jets that would blow away Smokey the Bear.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:32,000
Now, Xtreme Aircraft 2 on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
In Run Station 4 at Lockheed Martin in Fort Worth, Texas,

10
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,000
the future of aerial combat is preparing for flight.

11
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:13,720
This is the F-35A, a variant of the F-35 Lightning II,

12
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:17,620
arguably the most extreme fighter jet in the world.

13
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:21,660
With a fully loaded weight capacity of 68,000 pounds

14
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:25,300
and a 35-foot wingspan, the F-35A

15
00:01:25,301 --> 00:01:32,400
A-35A is capable of hitting a maximum cruising speed of Mach 1.6.

16
00:01:32,401 --> 00:01:39,140
That's fast enough to zip from LA to New York in about two hours.

17
00:01:39,141 --> 00:01:41,380
The muscle that powers this supersonic jet

18
00:01:41,381 --> 00:01:45,540
is the largest single turbofan ever put in a fighter,

19
00:01:45,541 --> 00:01:49,000
the Pratt & Whitney F-135 engine.

20
00:01:49,001 --> 00:01:50,580
Back here at the aft end of the airplane,

21
00:01:50,581 --> 00:01:52,420
we have the propulsion system.

22
00:01:52,421 --> 00:01:54,580
It's a single engine aircraft, one

23
00:01:54,581 --> 00:01:56,080
engines in a tactical fighter.

24
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:59,720
This particular engine has 40,000 pounds of thrust.

25
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:02,280
Essentially, in a 50,000-pound class aircraft,

26
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:03,680
it's going to be a pretty snappy performer.

27
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:09,420
To equal the force of the F-135,

28
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:12,740
you'd need the combined power of six top fuel dragsters

29
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:18,340
at 7,000 horsepower each, or the teamwork of 80 big rigs

30
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,080
with 500 horsepower engines.

31
00:02:20,081 --> 00:02:23,420
engines.

32
00:02:23,421 --> 00:02:25,880
The Lightning II also brings Ferrari-like finesse

33
00:02:25,881 --> 00:02:30,200
to its afterburner, essentially a system that adds raw fuel

34
00:02:30,201 --> 00:02:34,500
to the exhaust for a boost of explosive thrust.

35
00:02:34,501 --> 00:02:36,500
The afterburner, unlike previous engines

36
00:02:36,501 --> 00:02:39,000
where they stage through various levels of thrust,

37
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:42,560
it's one smooth acceleration.

38
00:02:42,561 --> 00:02:44,140
It's just an incredible amount of thrust.

39
00:02:44,141 --> 00:02:47,000
It's an incredible rush to put it in the upper left-hand corner

40
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:50,080
and pull back on the stick and let it go.

41
00:02:50,081 --> 00:02:57,080
The F-135 engine delivers the bronze, but it's the brains that

42
00:02:57,081 --> 00:03:01,080
set this warbird apart. Its onboard computer processor

43
00:03:01,081 --> 00:03:05,080
can run more than 1 trillion operations per second.

44
00:03:05,081 --> 00:03:08,080
A key feature of the F-35 Lightning II is the integrated

45
00:03:08,081 --> 00:03:12,080
sensor suite. It starts up front here, an active,

46
00:03:12,081 --> 00:03:15,080
electronically steered-away radar able to simultaneously track

47
00:03:15,081 --> 00:03:19,080
air-to-air and air-to-ground threats and engage them.

48
00:03:19,081 --> 00:03:21,840
As we move back a little bit, in this bay here,

49
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,880
will be an electro-optical sensor

50
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:27,320
able to track and engage targets on the ground

51
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:30,720
as well as in the air using optical means rather than radar.

52
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:31,920
Very, very good for stealth.

53
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,540
Prior to flight, every F-35A test pilot

54
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:41,840
puts in long hours on the simulator,

55
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:45,740
learning how to master the computer-integrated cockpit.

56
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,640
Some of the things that will really blow you away

57
00:03:47,641 --> 00:03:51,340
away. Inside the cockpit, it's almost like having a Windows

58
00:03:51,341 --> 00:03:57,480
environment. The cockpit has an 8 by 20 inch panoramic cockpit

59
00:03:57,481 --> 00:04:04,680
display, all AM LCD just like your TV at home, touch screen.

60
00:04:04,681 --> 00:04:08,000
Despite the user friendly interface, most pilots prefer to

61
00:04:08,001 --> 00:04:13,340
keep their eyes on the actual target. For this reason, the F-35A

62
00:04:13,341 --> 00:04:16,700
comes with a helmet that would satisfy the pickiest Star Wars

63
00:04:16,701 --> 00:04:19,700
fighter pilot.

64
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:24,040
This is the HMD, or helmet-mounted display.

65
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:27,580
When the visor is lowered, the pilot sees the skies,

66
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,880
as two electro-optical lenses project graphics directly

67
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:33,820
onto the visor.

68
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:35,520
Flight data and targeting information

69
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:38,720
are literally in your face.

70
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:40,760
No light, bad weather.

71
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:43,420
He can see what others can't, which is very compelling

72
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:45,860
if you're fighting in bad weather or at night.

73
00:04:45,861 --> 00:04:49,560
you can fight and see, and the others cannot.

74
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:51,800
The F-35A masterfully integrates

75
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,440
the extreme features that define the modern fighter,

76
00:04:55,540 --> 00:05:00,400
features pioneered by supersonic jets of the past.

77
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,880
Case in point, the Lightning II's modified delta wing.

78
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,420
It increases maneuverability because its leading edge

79
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:11,680
remains behind the shockwave generated by the nose

80
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:12,780
at supersonic speeds.

81
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:20,000
In the 1960s, the Soviets were the first to use the tailed

82
00:05:20,001 --> 00:05:23,600
delta wing on a supersonic fighter, on their MiG-21.

83
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:30,000
During the Vietnam War, the tailed delta wing enhanced the

84
00:05:30,001 --> 00:05:33,340
MiG's maneuverability, helping it level the playing field with

85
00:05:33,341 --> 00:05:36,080
the more powerful US F-4 Phantom II.

86
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:42,120
The much larger Phantom carried eight guided missiles, the MiG

87
00:05:42,121 --> 00:05:43,880
only two.

88
00:05:43,881 --> 00:05:46,440
But like a lethal gnat, the Russian-built jet

89
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:49,640
scored more than 40 air-to-air kills on US Phantoms.

90
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:56,540
It was an American fighter that pioneered another feature

91
00:05:56,541 --> 00:06:00,720
that's crucial to the Lightning II's extreme performance,

92
00:06:00,721 --> 00:06:04,600
its massive turbofan engine.

93
00:06:04,601 --> 00:06:07,500
Until the early 1960s, fighters came equipped

94
00:06:07,501 --> 00:06:08,720
with turbojet engines.

95
00:06:08,721 --> 00:06:14,020
turbojets inject fuel to ignite compressed air.

96
00:06:14,021 --> 00:06:18,020
The resulting combustion drives a turbine that produces thrust.

97
00:06:18,021 --> 00:06:22,960
The F-111 Aardvark, unveiled in 1964,

98
00:06:22,961 --> 00:06:26,180
was the first fighter with a turbofan engine,

99
00:06:26,181 --> 00:06:30,680
a design incorporating an external fan,

100
00:06:30,681 --> 00:06:34,520
similar to a propeller, that provides added thrust

101
00:06:34,521 --> 00:06:36,920
with airflow that bypasses the jet engine,

102
00:06:36,921 --> 00:06:42,560
engine, thus increasing power and fuel efficiency.

103
00:06:42,561 --> 00:06:47,160
The thrust of the F-111's two turbofans topped 50,000 pounds,

104
00:06:47,161 --> 00:06:50,100
three times the power of the first supersonic fighter,

105
00:06:50,101 --> 00:06:51,660
the F-100 Super Sabre.

106
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,380
A sheer power like this helps the F-35A dominate the skies.

107
00:06:59,381 --> 00:07:02,420
Its low observable profile, or stealth,

108
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:05,620
helps hide it from enemy radar.

109
00:07:05,621 --> 00:07:09,120
The first interceptor jet with extreme stealth capabilities

110
00:07:09,121 --> 00:07:14,380
was the YF-12, first flown in 1962.

111
00:07:14,381 --> 00:07:18,180
It evolved from the same program that spawned the SR-71 Blackbird

112
00:07:18,181 --> 00:07:20,520
spy plane.

113
00:07:20,521 --> 00:07:23,080
Each plane's wings featured surface angles designed

114
00:07:23,081 --> 00:07:26,440
to absorb and reflect radar, which significantly reduced

115
00:07:26,441 --> 00:07:27,560
their radar signatures.

116
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:33,040
The Lightning II's classified stealth design

117
00:07:33,041 --> 00:07:37,780
to reduce its signature to the size of a golf ball.

118
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:40,280
But stealth design isn't the only asset contained in the

119
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:42,480
Lightning II's airframe.

120
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:46,480
It's also compact, lightweight, and nimble,

121
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:51,860
aerodynamic pluses pioneered in 1976 by the F-16 Fighting

122
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:54,500
Falcon.

123
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:58,640
The Falcon's power, combined with its sleek, small design,

124
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:01,140
changed the physics of aerial combat,

125
00:08:01,141 --> 00:08:04,640
enabling pilots to turn more rapidly inside an opponent

126
00:08:04,740 --> 00:08:06,680
and lock missiles.

127
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:09,640
The Falcon was the first fighter rated at sustained forces

128
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:14,620
nine times the force of gravity, or 9 Gs, the threshold at which

129
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:18,620
all but the most seasoned pilots would black out.

130
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:22,120
Most jet fighters before the F-16 were rated at only 6 Gs.

131
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:28,140
At 9 Gs, a 200-pound pilot weighs

132
00:08:28,141 --> 00:08:31,340
weighs the equivalent of 1,800 pounds.

133
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:35,140
The attributes of the F-16 were simply undeniable.

134
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:39,080
And over a period of time, over 4,000 F-16s were built.

135
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:41,080
And in fact, the type is still in production.

136
00:08:44,900 --> 00:08:48,500
A newer jet fighter that began production in 2003,

137
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:51,600
the F-22 Raptor, featured the final element

138
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:54,540
that became an integral part of the Lightning II.

139
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:58,140
The Raptor came equipped with advanced integrated avionics.

140
00:08:58,141 --> 00:09:01,780
complex computer software that runs flight controls, weapons,

141
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:05,640
and other onboard systems, freeing the pilot to concentrate

142
00:09:05,641 --> 00:09:09,180
on the battle. The combination of the F-22

143
00:09:09,181 --> 00:09:13,500
and the F-35 together can tear down the walls of an opponent,

144
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:16,300
open up the sky above them, and then make that opponent more

145
00:09:16,301 --> 00:09:18,280
vulnerable to conventional attacks.

146
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:27,620
America's future fighter, the F-35 Lightning II,

147
00:09:27,621 --> 00:09:31,220
represents the culmination of 70-plus years of fighter trial

148
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:32,020
and error.

149
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:38,060
In fact, the F-35 is three fighters in one.

150
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:43,360
It was originally called the Joint Strike Fighter

151
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:46,080
because it was designed to jointly serve three branches

152
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:46,880
of the military.

153
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:53,580
Lockheed Martin will deliver the conventional F-35A

154
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:54,580
to the Air Force.

155
00:09:57,620 --> 00:10:01,520
The F-35B is a short takeoff and vertical landing fighter

156
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:04,960
designed for the Marine Corps, first demonstrated

157
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:09,260
as the X-35B prototype.

158
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:12,860
The F-35C is an aircraft carrier launch version

159
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:15,180
for the US Navy.

160
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:17,880
We built three variants of this airplane,

161
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:19,380
basically off the same airplane,

162
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:21,080
kind of like the way car industry

163
00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:24,280
builds a family of SUVs and pickup trucks

164
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:25,380
off the same chassis.

165
00:10:27,620 --> 00:10:32,420
Lockheed Martin's Fort Worth production facility is

166
00:10:32,520 --> 00:10:37,000
responsible for crafting all F-35 wings and forward fuselages.

167
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:44,000
The crucial component on the fuselage of the F-35B is the

168
00:10:44,100 --> 00:10:46,340
vertical lift fan.

169
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:49,500
And the key to this fifth generation airplane is just

170
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:50,740
above my head.

171
00:10:50,840 --> 00:10:53,380
And what this is is the lift fan area.

172
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:55,920
These are the veins that direct the airflow.

173
00:10:55,921 --> 00:10:58,920
There's 19,000 pounds of thrust that will come out of this,

174
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:00,920
19,000 pounds in the rear.

175
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:02,920
We have roll posts in the wing.

176
00:11:03,020 --> 00:11:05,520
They'll get about 1,900 pounds thrust each.

177
00:11:05,620 --> 00:11:08,560
And that allows this to come in for the vertical landing,

178
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:11,660
also the short takeoff.

179
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:15,580
It has doors on top that open up like an old 55 Chevy hood.

180
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,180
The lift fan engages.

181
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:20,580
So at 250 knots, the pilot comes in, engages the lift fan,

182
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:21,680
opens the doors.

183
00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:24,080
The plane begins to slow down, transitions

184
00:11:24,081 --> 00:11:27,120
from airborne, wing-borne flight to jet-borne flight,

185
00:11:27,220 --> 00:11:29,220
and can almost, you know, land like a helicopter.

186
00:11:32,220 --> 00:11:36,160
The F-35 is a group effort, with the United States

187
00:11:36,260 --> 00:11:38,020
joining eight foreign nation partners.

188
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:45,960
The various F-35 versions will have a commonality of parts,

189
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:49,080
making the airframe far less expensive to build and fly

190
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:50,580
than the F-22 Raptor.

191
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:58,320
The F-35 represents the largest aerospace defense program

192
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:00,380
in history.

193
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:04,320
But some say it could be the fighter pilot's last ride.

194
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:07,720
I would propose to this program,

195
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:12,400
as we sit here today, that the F-35 potentially represents

196
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:14,800
the last manned fighter.

197
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:18,440
If it's not the end of the road for the manned fighter,

198
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:20,400
it's getting awfully darn close.

199
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:29,060
Flying state-of-the-art extreme fighters like the F-35 is one way to attack the enemy.

200
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:41,820
Another is to load 25 tons of bombs into an aircraft with the engine power of three F-35s.

201
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:51,320
The F-35 Lightning II is nearly 40% carbon fiber composite by weight,

202
00:12:51,321 --> 00:12:54,920
the most of any fighter in history.

203
00:12:55,020 --> 00:12:58,920
Extreme Aircraft II will return on Modern Marvels.

204
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:07,360
We now return to Extreme Aircraft II on Modern Marvels.

205
00:13:07,361 --> 00:13:08,360
marbles.

206
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:23,340
At Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota,

207
00:13:23,341 --> 00:13:25,920
the airmen of the 28th Bomb Wing are gearing up to go

208
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:29,620
supersonic once again.

209
00:13:29,621 --> 00:13:30,980
Their mission?

210
00:13:30,981 --> 00:13:33,020
Take out a runway and some buildings

211
00:13:33,021 --> 00:13:34,420
in southeastern Montana.

212
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:39,360
With orders in hand, they make the commute to the flight

213
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:40,160
line.

214
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:46,360
Their extreme aircraft is a bomber

215
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:47,460
that thinks it's a fighter.

216
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:55,120
It's called the B-1B Lancer.

217
00:13:55,220 --> 00:13:58,920
The men call it the bone.

218
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:04,960
The B-1B bomber is 146 feet long, weighs approximately 440,000

219
00:14:04,961 --> 00:14:09,500
pounds fully loaded and can reach supersonic speeds of Mach 1.2.

220
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:20,160
The four General Electric turbofan engines produce 30,000 pounds of thrust each,

221
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:27,100
meaning the B-1 is powered by the equivalent of three F-35 fighter engines.

222
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:30,960
The B-1 is a great aircraft to flight.

223
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:39,400
We have the speed and the capability to make hard turns.

224
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:42,200
Transition from high altitude to low altitude very quickly

225
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:43,200
in a dive.

226
00:14:43,300 --> 00:14:46,200
We have the speed and the legs to really just kind of turn

227
00:14:46,300 --> 00:14:47,100
and burn.

228
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:54,720
One hour into the mission, the crew's target

229
00:14:54,820 --> 00:14:57,320
is within striking distance.

230
00:14:57,420 --> 00:15:00,620
In the aft compartment, the offensive systems officer

231
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:04,960
prepares to unleash the bomber's fearsome payload.

232
00:15:04,961 --> 00:15:07,860
A sensitive radar with ground-moving target tracking

233
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:10,860
helps pinpoint the target for destruction.

234
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:13,760
What we're looking at, you got a road running basically

235
00:15:13,860 --> 00:15:14,860
north-south.

236
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:16,100
You got your runway here.

237
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:17,300
And these bright returns right there

238
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:19,400
are the buildings that we'll be striking today.

239
00:15:22,220 --> 00:15:25,220
Right here, it's telling me I got 56 seconds to release.

240
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:27,620
And I got three 2,000-pound weapons coming out

241
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:28,620
on those buildings.

242
00:15:28,621 --> 00:15:35,620
Doors, board and app coming open.

243
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:38,460
Right here, you can see the picture of the doors open.

244
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:42,460
One away, two away, three, three away.

245
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:44,060
Doors coming closed.

246
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:48,480
System safe, clear to maneuver.

247
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:52,280
In this training mission over the farm fields of Montana,

248
00:15:52,380 --> 00:15:53,680
the bombs are simulations.

249
00:15:58,620 --> 00:16:03,320
But another day, this same crew may repeat this methodical and

250
00:16:03,321 --> 00:16:10,360
deadly process for real. If that happens, the B-1B will

251
00:16:10,361 --> 00:16:16,300
bring an overwhelming payload to the battle.

252
00:16:16,301 --> 00:16:21,040
Its three massive bomb bays carry a flexible arsenal.

253
00:16:21,041 --> 00:16:25,320
This includes smart weapons, bombs and guided missiles that

