666
00:43:22,760 --> 00:43:26,060
The air tanker itself doesn't put out fires.

667
00:43:26,061 --> 00:43:29,200
not an air tanker around that will, especially on large fires.

668
00:43:29,201 --> 00:43:30,800
But what this does is it allows them

669
00:43:30,801 --> 00:43:32,660
to work very closely to the line.

670
00:43:32,661 --> 00:43:33,600
They can build line.

671
00:43:33,601 --> 00:43:36,740
They can reinforce it.

672
00:43:36,741 --> 00:43:39,480
The pilot can control the density of the drop

673
00:43:39,481 --> 00:43:43,020
by adjusting altitude and varying the width of the opening

674
00:43:43,021 --> 00:43:46,440
on the tank doors.

675
00:43:46,441 --> 00:43:50,220
A typical 12,000-gallon drop from 250 feet

676
00:43:50,221 --> 00:43:54,020
covers an unbroken swath that's approximately 50 feet wide

677
00:43:54,021 --> 00:43:57,420
and 3 quarters of a mile long.

678
00:43:57,520 --> 00:43:59,820
To build a stronger, denser line,

679
00:43:59,920 --> 00:44:02,720
a pilot can increase flow rate for a SWAT that's approximately

680
00:44:02,820 --> 00:44:04,800
half a mile long.

681
00:44:04,900 --> 00:44:07,200
We can do it in a matter of minutes and seconds

682
00:44:07,300 --> 00:44:11,440
that it would take us hours to do with other aircraft.

683
00:44:11,540 --> 00:44:13,880
The DC-10 represents yet another attempt

684
00:44:13,980 --> 00:44:16,440
to apply extreme aircraft solutions

685
00:44:16,540 --> 00:44:17,880
